course: dictionary
lesson: 512
title: Assimilate: taking in and becoming part
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Assimilate is a verb, and it has three main meanings. In each one, something new is taken in and becomes part of a person, a group, or a body. Its forms are assimilate, assimilates, assimilated and assimilating.
explain: Read the context clues. «Ukrainians there had parties and cooperatives but were pressed to assimilate (give up their own language and ways).» The brackets explain the word, and the word «pressed» shows that people were pushed into it, not that they chose it freely.
explain: The word also works in learning and science. You assimilate facts when you take them in and understand them fully. Plants assimilate carbon, which means they take it in and make it part of their bodies.
fact: Assimilate means to take something in and make it part of you or your group.
fact: «Assimilate into» means to become part of a new group, community or country, often by taking on its ways.
fact: «Assimilate information» means to take in new facts and understand them fully.
fact: In science, plants assimilate carbon, and animals assimilate food, making it part of the body.
fact: Forced assimilation, where people are pressed to give up their own language and ways, is often seen as unfair.
exercise: «Ukrainians there ... were pressed to assimilate (give up their own language and ways).» What does assimilate mean here? | To declare independence for one day | *To become part of a new country, giving up their own language and ways | To move up into the mountains | To hold their own parties and meetings
exercise: Mia reads a long article about volcanoes and wants to fully understand every fact. Which word fits best? | *Assimilate | Whisper | Drooped | Forget
exercise: In science, plants take in carbon and make it part of their bodies. Plants ______ carbon. Which word fits the gap? | *Assimilate | Dismiss | Ignore | Drown
test: A family moves abroad and slowly learns the language and customs, becoming part of their new community. Which word fits? | *Assimilate | Evaporate | Hibernate | Celebrate
test: Why is forced assimilation often seen as unfair? | It means nothing to anyone | *It takes away people's own language and ways | It makes people richer straight away | It gives people more parties
exam: In «assimilate information», what does assimilate mean? | To throw information away | To write information on a wall | To shout information loudly | *To take in and understand new information fully
